Executive View
CMS converted two historically weak controls into measurable 2028 obligations.
Medicare Advantage plans would face recurring provider-directory verification=
 and public accuracy grades, while Part D PBMs would face cost-based compensa=
tion, affiliate reporting, audit and disgorgement requirements. The immediate=
 effect is implementation demand; the larger economic effect is potential red=
istribution of vertically integrated pharmacy revenue. (
cms.gov
)
UnitedHealth=E2=80=99s recovery is now visibly volume-for-margin rather than =
volume-led.
UnitedHealthcare improved profitability while shedding 935,000 Medicare Advan=
tage members since year-end; Optum Health similarly restored margin while exi=
ting value-based-care markets lacking sufficient density. (
sec.gov
)
Humana found a less balance-sheet-intensive structure for retaining operation=
al control of CenterWell clinics.
BlackRock will majority-own nearly 150 clinics, but CenterWell will continue =
managing them; removal of the legacy put obligation materially changes Humana=
=E2=80=99s capital exposure. (
humana.gcs-web.com
)
Alignment=E2=80=99s litigation makes part of the newly disclosed Stars diverg=
ence legally contingent.
The suit does not restore 2028 quality-bonus revenue, but it creates a plausi=
ble recalculation path and complicates benefit and capital planning.
Overall environment:
improved for near-term payer margins and provider-asset capital flexibility; =
deteriorated for PBM and provider-directory compliance burden; broadly unchan=
ged in aggregate.
1. CMS makes provider data and PBM affiliate economics auditable
What happened
On October 16, CMS proposed implementing provisions of the Consolidated Appro=
priations Act of 2026 for contract year 2028. Medicare Advantage organization=
s would have to verify most provider-directory listings at least every 90 day=
s, conduct annual accuracy analyses using random samples and publish plan-lev=
el letter grades. Hospitals and specified facilities would generally receive =
annual verification. Enrollees misled by inaccurate listings would pay no mor=
e than in-network cost sharing. Public, machine-readable accuracy scores woul=
d follow in 2029. (CMS,
Medicare Program; Implementation of Sections 6219, 6220, and 6224 of the Cons=
olidated Appropriations Act, 2026
, October 16, 2026.) (
cms.gov
)
The Part D provisions are more economically significant. PBMs and affiliates =
generally could retain only bona fide service fees, with CMS proposing fair-m=
arket-value compensation based on the cost of an itemized service plus a reas=
onable markup=E2=80=94not drug price. PBMs would report compensation, guarant=
ees, acquisition costs, pharmacy activity and affiliate arrangements; plan sp=
onsors would gain audit rights, and prohibited remuneration could be disgorge=
d through the sponsor to CMS. Annual reporting would begin no later than July=
 1, 2028. (Federal Register,
CMS-4214-P
, public inspection October 16, 2026; scheduled publication October 20, 2026.=
) (
public-inspection.federalregister.gov
)
Implication
Provider directories are moving from lightly governed consumer content to an =
audited master-data function. Plans will need identity resolution, roster rec=
onciliation, provider outreach, sampling, evidence retention and defensible s=
coring across credentialing, contracting and FHIR-based directory infrastruct=
ure. That favors shared provider-data utilities, but switching costs will rem=
ain high because the liability sits with the plan.
For vertically integrated PBMs, the rule attacks ambiguity across rebate aggr=
egation, specialty-pharmacy acquisition economics, administrative fees and af=
filiate transfers. The direction is toward explicit service pricing and spons=
or-level visibility. The P&L magnitude remains uncertain until CMS finalizes =
definitions of cost and =E2=80=9Creasonable markup,=E2=80=9D but contract rem=
ediation must begin well before 2028.
Watch next
Whether CMS modifies the proposed cost-plus definition of fair market value a=
nd how it treats services performed across PBM, specialty-pharmacy, group-pur=
chasing and plan affiliates.
2. UnitedHealth demonstrates that selective contraction can repair margins
What happened
UnitedHealth reported an 88.4% third-quarter medical-care ratio, versus 89.9%=
 one year earlier, including $610 million of favorable reserve development. U=
nitedHealthcare=E2=80=99s operating margin rose to 3.3% from 2.1%, reflecting=
 benefit changes, pricing and medical-cost management. Medicare Advantage enr=
ollment, however, contracted by 935,000 members from year-end 2025. (UnitedHe=
alth Group,
Third Quarter 2026 Results
, October 13, 2026.) (
sec.gov
)
Optum Health revenue declined 10% year over year because it reduced value-bas=
ed-care membership in markets where it lacked sufficient scale, while operati=
ng margin improved to 3.2% from 0.2%. Optum Insight revenue grew 3%, and oper=
ating earnings increased to $1.28 billion from $920 million, which management=
 attributed to product enhancements and operating efficiencies. (
sec.gov
)
Implication
The results resolve an important operating question: UnitedHealth=E2=80=99s r=
etrenchment is producing margin recovery, but through narrower participation =
and greater local density=E2=80=94not broad membership growth. Competitors sh=
ould expect continued discipline in MA benefit design and provider contractin=
g during the current enrollment cycle.
For provider groups, the Optum Health result reinforces that value-based-care=
 capital will concentrate in markets where an operator can align sufficient a=
ttributed lives, clinical capacity and pharmacy or specialty services. For he=
alth-technology vendors, the evidence is stronger for demand tied to cost tak=
eout, payment accuracy and clinician productivity than for indiscriminate exp=
ansion of risk-bearing care platforms.
Watch next
Whether 2027 enrollment stabilizes without reversing the margin gains, and wh=
ether Optum resumes value-based-care growth only in markets where UnitedHealt=
hcare or external payer volume provides adequate density.
3. Humana retains clinic control while transferring more capital risk
What happened
Humana announced on October 13 that BlackRock=E2=80=99s HPS Investment Partne=
rs will acquire Welsh Carson=E2=80=99s approximately $2.8 billion interest in=
 joint ventures holding nearly 150 CenterWell Senior Primary Care clinics. Bl=
ackRock will own a majority interest; CenterWell will retain a minority stake=
 and continue managing all clinics. The venture expects to fund existing oper=
ations, new clinics and acquisitions over the next two years. (
humana.gcs-web.com
)
Unlike the legacy structure, the new venture has no put option requiring Huma=
na to repurchase the investor=E2=80=99s position. CenterWell may elect to pur=
chase clinics beginning in 2028; if clinics remain in 2033, BlackRock may ini=
tiate a sale process subject to CenterWell=E2=80=99s first-offer right.
Implication
Humana is separating operational control from asset ownership. It preserves t=
he clinical platform supporting MA care management and risk economics while l=
owering balance-sheet liabilities and the cost of expansion capital. That mak=
es continued clinic growth more feasible following Humana=E2=80=99s Stars rec=
overy without committing future quality-bonus revenue to real-estate and clin=
ic-development obligations.
The trade-off is longer-term ownership uncertainty. Humana retains management=
 and optionality, but eventual purchase or sale decisions could affect where =
care-delivery economics ultimately accrue.
Watch next
The amount and deployment of BlackRock=E2=80=99s additional capital, particul=
arly whether growth favors de novo clinics or acquisitions in markets aligned=
 with Humana=E2=80=99s strongest MA contracts.
4. Alignment turns its Stars loss into a regulatory-methodology challenge
What happened
Alignment filed suit on October 9 challenging its 2027 Star Ratings, after it=
s California HMO contract=E2=80=94serving approximately 75% of company member=
ship=E2=80=94fell from four stars to 3.5 stars. The complaint argues that CMS=
 continued using measures not properly adopted through notice-and-comment reg=
ulation, drawing on the earlier Clover decision. (Alignment Healthcare, Form =
8-K, October 8, 2026; Healthcare Dive,
Alignment sues CMS over 2027 Medicare Advantage star ratings crash
, October 13, 2026.) (
sec.gov
)
Implication
Last week=E2=80=99s Stars divergence is no longer solely a quality-execution =
issue; for affected contracts, it now includes legal option value. A successf=
ul expedited challenge could restore bonus economics, but no recalculation ha=
s occurred and Alignment must still address the underlying HOS and Part D per=
formance weaknesses it identified.
Plans facing disputed methodology need parallel 2028 scenarios rather than as=
suming displayed ratings are either fully final or likely to be overturned. R=
eliable public timing for a merits decision was unavailable as of October 19.
Watch next
Whether the court accelerates review before 2028 bid development and whether =
additional carriers challenge the same measures.
What I'm Watching Next
October 20, 2026:
CMS-4214-P is scheduled for publication in the
Federal Register
. (
public-inspection.federalregister.gov
)
December 15, 2026, at 5 p.m. EST:
Comments on CMS-4214-P are due. (
public-inspection.federalregister.gov
)
Bottom Line
The week=E2=80=99s most important change is CMS=E2=80=99s conversion of MA pr=
ovider-directory accuracy and Part D PBM economics into auditable, enforceabl=
e operating controls. Vertically integrated PBM affiliate compensation requir=
es the closest strategic attention, while provider-data governance is the cle=
arest near-term technology implementation opportunity. The December 15 commen=
t deadline is the next confirmed event most likely to alter the rule=E2=80=99=
s economic impact.
